":"ADJ","personal data":"none","postal addresses":"none","programming language":"Markdown","source":"pile-uncopyrighted","toxicity":"none"},"text":"Schaffler JL, Tremblay S, Laizner AM, Lambert S. Developing education materials for caregivers of culturally and linguistically diverse patients: Insights from a qualitative analysis of caregivers\\' needs, access and understanding of information. Health Expect. 2019;22:444--456. 10.1111/hex.12867 30767349\n\n**Funding information**\n\nThis work was supported by Quebec Nursing Intervention Research Network (RISIIQ) \\[Grant number \\#4703\\].\n\n1. BACKGROUND {#hex12867-sec-0006}\n=============\n\nAs many as 60% of Canadians are diagnosed with a chronic illness, many of whom are from a culturally and linguistically diverse (CALD) background.[1](#hex12867-bib-0001){ref-type=\"ref\"} Culturally and linguistically diverse refers to individuals who are foreign born, whose main language spoken at home is neither English nor French, who are proficient in neither English nor French, and have non‐Indigenous status.[2](#hex12867-bib-0002){ref-type=\"ref\"} Culturally and linguistically diverse populations include both immigrants as well as persons born outside of Canada with Canadian citizenship. Indigenous persons are excluded due to their unique experiences and needs as first nation people, which differ significantly from other minority groups.[3](#hex12867-bib-0003){ref-type=\"ref\"}\n\nIn Canada, 20.6% of the population is foreign‐born. One concern is that the incidence of chronic illness is higher among CALD populations compared to the general population.[4](#hex12867-bib-0004){ref-type=\"ref\"} Despite this, access and usage of health‐care services among CALD populations are low.[5](#hex12867-bib-0005){ref-type=\"ref\"} This may be related to the unique challenges faced by these communities including unfamiliarity with the health‐care system, a lack of culturally and linguistically appropriate information, and being unable to communicate effectively with the treating team.[6](#hex12867-bib-0006){ref-type=\"ref\"} These barriers may also in part account for the findings that CALD individuals often have a poor understanding of their condition and associated medical treatments.[4](#hex12867-bib-0004){ref-type=\"ref\"}\n\nLiving with a chronic illness requires that patients engage in self‐management activities to enhance health outcomes such as symptom management, pain control and role functioning.[7](#hex12867-bib-0007){ref-type=\"ref\"} Adams et al[8](#hex12867-bib-0008){ref-type=\"ref\"} described self‐management as the tasks that an individual must undertake to live well with one or more chronic conditions. To engage effectively in self‐management, patients must develop five core skills, including problem‐solving, action planning, decision making, locating and utilizing resources and forming partnerships with health providers.[9](#hex12867-bib-0009){ref-type=\"ref\"} These skills facilitate the medical, emotional and role management of chronic illness,[9](#hex12867-bib-0009){ref-type=\"ref\"} which in turn leads to improved health.[10](#hex12867-bib-0010){ref-type=\"ref\"} Nonetheless, CAL","tokens":["Sch","aff","ler"," J","L",","," T","rem","bl","ay"," S",","," La","iz","ner"," AM",","," Lambert"," S","."," Develop","ing"," education"," materials"," for"," caregivers"," of"," culturally"," and"," lingu","istically"," diverse"," patients",":"," Ins","ights"," from"," a"," qualitative"," analysis"," of"," caregivers","\\'"," needs",","," access"," and"," understanding"," of"," information","."," Health"," Expect","."," 2019",";","22",":","444","--","456","."," 10",".","1111","/","hex",".","128","67"," 307","67","349","\n","\n","**","Funding"," information","**","\n","\n","This"," work"," was"," supported"," by"," Quebec"," Nursing"," Inter","vention"," Research"," Network"," (","R","IS","II","Q",")"," \\[","Grant"," number"," \\#","4","703","\\].","\n","\n","1","."," BACKGROUND"," {#",